01:45.913 --> 01:49.251
or we can say that our second derivative,

01:49.251 --> 01:53.333
our second derivative is less than zero.

01:53.333 --> 01:55.481
And once again, I can draw it on this.

01:55.481 --> 01:58.902
So, when x is lower, we have a, look,

01:58.902 --> 02:00.749
or it looks like we have a positive slope,

02:00.749 --> 02:02.411
then it becomes less positive,

02:02.411 --> 02:03.549
and then it becomes less positive,

02:03.549 --> 02:05.020
as it's approaching zero, it becomes zero,

02:05.020 --> 02:07.723
then it becomes negative,
then even more negative,

02:07.723 --> 02:09.326
and then even more negative.

02:09.326 --> 02:11.767
So as you can see, our slope
is constantly decreasing

02:11.767 --> 02:14.209
as x increases here.

02:14.209 --> 02:15.787
So in order to think about the intervals

02:15.787 --> 02:20.008
where g is either concave
upward or concave downward,

02:20.008 --> 02:23.173
what we need to do is let's
find the second derivative of g,

02:23.173 --> 02:24.232
